WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:03.294
[whimsical music]

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:05.171
I'm just going to come out 
and say it.

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:07.382
I think bats are 
super weird.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.051
I mean that in an endearing way.

00:00:10.093 --> 00:00:11.761
I actually like them a lot.

00:00:11.803 --> 00:00:13.847
I think they're these like fluffy,
flying mammals

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:16.349
with a lot to offer, 
like tequila.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:19.477
They pollinate the agave plant.

00:00:21.104 --> 00:00:22.897
The trouble is, when bats 
hit the media

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:25.900
it’s almost always over 
some deadly disease

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.901
like Ebola...

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.070
or COVID-19...

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:32.782
and even the recent Nipah virus 
outbreaks in India.

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:35.160
Bats are frequently taking 
the blame.

00:00:35.618 --> 00:00:37.620
And yet we're not finding caves

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.874
full of bats that died from 
coronavirus or Ebola...

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:43.752
because bats don't just host 
deadly diseases.

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:45.879
They seem to tolerate them really well.

00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:48.590
Researchers think it's because 
they evolved

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:49.799
with a unique ability.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.344
They're the only mammals 
that can fly.

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:04.355
So there’s a number of unique features 
of bat immune systems

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:07.233
and their physiology that we think 
have co-evolved

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.235
with the evolution of flight.

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:12.614
Flying requires an incredible 
amount of energy.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:17.285
A bat in flight elevates its basal 
metabolic rate up to 15 fold.

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:20.914
And to put that in context, a human
at full speed running

00:01:20.914 --> 00:01:23.208
is only doubling their metabolic rate.

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:26.336
And a rodent, perhaps, elevating it 
seven fold.

00:01:26.336 --> 00:01:29.255
So this is more than twice 
the energetic expenditure...

00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:31.716
of any form of terrestrial locomotion.

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:33.635
Their heart rates can skyrocket

00:01:33.635 --> 00:01:35.428
to over a thousand 
beats per minute.

00:01:35.678 --> 00:01:39.099
That's around 4 or 5 times more 
than their resting heart rate.

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:41.351
But there's even more.

00:01:41.684 --> 00:01:44.312
It's honestly remarkable that 
when a bat takes flight

00:01:44.312 --> 00:01:46.564
it doesn't just drop dead.

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:49.692
So a bat in flight will elevate 
its body temperature

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:51.736
up to 44 degrees centigrade.

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:54.948
And 45 degrees centigrade is 
kind of the lethal limit

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:57.158
of what mammals can tolerate.

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:58.993
And that holds true for bats as well.

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:00.370
So when in flight

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.997
they're really pushing the sort of 
physiological limits...

00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:05.500
of thermal tolerance.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:08.753
And while all of this is fascinating,
you're probably wondering...

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.005
what does this have to do 
with viruses?

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:13.758
Well, stick with me because 
we are getting there.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:18.847
So flight is essentially a huge stressor 
that resembles aging...

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:22.684
and because it's a part of bats’ 
innate physiology

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:25.103
they’ve evolved unique 
molecular pathways

00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:27.897
to mitigate that damage 
at the site of production.

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:30.733
So essentially, bat physiology 
is resilient

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:32.110
to the process of aging.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:35.905
You see, despite burning a ton of energy
all of the time

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:37.949
bats are shockingly long lived.

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:40.326
Usually there are trade-offs between 

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.288
metabolic output and lifespan

00:02:43.288 --> 00:02:45.081
and rodents are a great example of that.

00:02:45.456 --> 00:02:47.959
Typically, they only live up to 
a couple of years.

00:02:48.293 --> 00:02:50.461
Bats, however, are about the same size

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:52.797
and have metabolic expenditure

00:02:52.797 --> 00:02:55.175
that even exceeds that of rodents.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:56.634
But they're extremely long lived.

00:02:56.634 --> 00:02:59.554
The oldest known that lives up to 
40 years in the wild.

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:02.599
So after actually correcting 
for body size

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:04.726
bats are the longest lived mammals 
on Earth.

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:07.270
So these special pathways 
that they've developed

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:10.064
in order to mitigate the intense 
effects of flying

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:13.193
have likely made them extremely 
tolerant to viruses...

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:17.030
or at least primed them to 
successfully fight them off.

00:03:17.322 --> 00:03:18.990
[percussive, pensive music]

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.618
When a virus enters a human, 
two things happen.

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:25.622
Okay, more than two things happened.

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:28.333
But let's focus on two, because 
who has the time?

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:31.419
Think of these like two switches 
that need to be turned on

00:03:31.419 --> 00:03:33.087
when a virus infects a cell...

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.757
and off when the body 
is clear of the virus.

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:38.009
So when a virus shows up

00:03:38.009 --> 00:03:40.929
it enters a cell and your body 
raises an alarm like:

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:44.849
Hey, there's a guy here who's not even 
supposed to be here!

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:47.310
That flips the alarm switch...

00:03:47.435 --> 00:03:49.229
which releases some molecules

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.231
that slow the virus from replicating.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:54.192
And then the second switch flips

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:57.779
which launches an immune response 
to kill off the rest of the virus.

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:00.281
But there's a catch.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:02.909
Once that immune response switches on

00:04:02.909 --> 00:04:06.996
our bodies can sometimes start to 
experience some pretty severe symptoms...

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:11.125
like a fever, body aches, and 
essentially inflammation...

00:04:11.668 --> 00:04:16.422
which, sure, fights the virus
but also...

00:04:16.422 --> 00:04:18.216
The immune response causes

00:04:18.216 --> 00:04:20.176
widespread inflammation in the body

00:04:20.176 --> 00:04:22.178
which is and of itself highly fatal.

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:25.974
But bats can't have their body 
triggering inflammation

00:04:25.974 --> 00:04:28.851
every time they experience 
an extreme stressor...

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:32.480
because their entire existence 
as a flying mammal

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.565
is basically an extreme stressor.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:37.485
And so their switch responses 
to an invader

00:04:37.485 --> 00:04:39.195
 work a bit differently 
than ours.

00:04:40.029 --> 00:04:43.491
So in some species their alarm switch 
is perpetually on.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.995
In other species, it's entirely 
in overdrive...

00:04:47.203 --> 00:04:48.663
which means they're sounding an alarm

00:04:48.663 --> 00:04:51.916
and releasing molecules to stop a virus 
from replicating

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:53.918
before it even enters their body.

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:57.046
And their switch that triggers inflammation

00:04:57.046 --> 00:04:58.923
kind of has a permanent dimmer.

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:01.426
So they don't feel these 
strong side effects.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:05.555
Bats are particularly adept at 
reducing viral loads

00:05:05.555 --> 00:05:09.225
to very low levels and simply tolerating
whatever is remaining.

00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:13.062
Which might be part of the reason 
why the viruses they do host

00:05:13.062 --> 00:05:15.148
are so much more dangerous 
to humans.

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:18.818
The longer a virus stays in a host, 
the more chances it has

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:22.196
to replicate, mutate, jump to a 
new bat or animal

00:05:22.196 --> 00:05:24.949
and ultimately becomes something more deadly.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.203
But before you start thinking 
about bats as these

00:05:29.203 --> 00:05:31.748
horrible, disease ridden 
flying creeps...

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.751
it's worth noting there is a lot 
we can learn from them...

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:38.296
including how to better treat 
disease in humans.

00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:40.715
At least one group of researchers in Dublin

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:43.384
recently received a grant to study just that.

00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:44.969
And some of our methods

00:05:44.969 --> 00:05:46.929
already work to mimic 
the switches in a bat...

00:05:47.138 --> 00:05:49.807
including how we treat many 
severe COVID cases.

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:52.602
We first treat patients 
with Remdesivir

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:56.022
which is an antiviral drug that 
tries to reduce the viral load...

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:59.734
but then later on in infections 
we’ll treat with dexamethasone

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:00.943
which is a steroid...

00:06:01.152 --> 00:06:04.238
that's meant to actually blunt 
that immunopathology.

00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:06.032
So it's anti-inflammatory.

00:06:06.657 --> 00:06:09.869
And so we turn the alarm on
and we dim the other switch.

00:06:11.329 --> 00:06:13.664
Of course, bats aren't invincible.

00:06:13.956 --> 00:06:17.377
And recent studies have shown that 
bats under extreme stress...

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.796
caused by things like rising 
global temperatures

00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:21.964
habitat loss and human interference...

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:25.218
are more likely to shed 
and spread viruses.

00:06:25.676 --> 00:06:28.596
So I feel strongly that there is 
a win-win argument

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:31.307
for bat conservation and 
human public health...

00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:34.936
because a healthy bat is simply less 
of a zoonotic threat

00:06:34.936 --> 00:06:36.646
to humans than as a sick bat.

00:06:37.063 --> 00:06:40.191
While that does pose zoonotic risk

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:43.486
they also offer a really valuable...

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:47.865
research source for new approaches 
in medicine.

00:06:48.991 --> 00:06:50.535
Hey everybody, thanks for watching.

00:06:50.535 --> 00:06:54.414
I'm Kim, the producer of this episode,
and there is just so much

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:55.998
we did not get to cover in this.

00:06:55.998 --> 00:06:59.460
Including how a broad diversity
of species of bats

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.423
might also contribute to why diseases 
they carry are so deadly...

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:06.384
or even just the things that do affect bats...

00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:08.052
like white nose fungus.

00:07:08.302 --> 00:07:10.513
There's a long list of topics to explore

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:12.557
and this piece is really just 
meant to be a primer.

00:07:12.557 --> 00:07:14.308
But hopefully it makes you 
want to learn more.

00:07:14.642 --> 00:07:16.769
I've included a bunch of links 
in the description

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:18.062
to help you get started.

00:07:18.354 --> 00:07:20.356
Before you go, I do have a request.

00:07:20.565 --> 00:07:22.567
